  "actual_question": "How do light-dependent reactions provide the energy carriers used by the Calvin cycle?",
  "answer_id": "29924da6-426e-49f4-ac2e-0b6ddbc6688e",
  "request_id": "1feec7e6-55ce-45e1-9004-3759a38d90a7",
  "job_id": "575eb62a-2d3c-4bb3-b632-6ed2a022b8c1",
  "final_response": {
    "schema_version": "chat_response_v1",
    "response_type": "answer",
    "answer_text": "The light-dependent reactions convert light energy into chemical energy [ev_008], and the energy carriers then move that energy to the Calvin cycle [ev_008]. The light-dependent reactions use light to temporarily store energy in energy carriers [ev_005] [ev_008]. Photosystems absorb light and use electron transport chains to convert energy into the chemical energy of ATP and NADPH [ev_002]. The energy carriers that move energy from the light-dependent reactions to the Calvin cycle are described as \"full\" because they bring energy [ev_001] [ev_005]. These energy-carrying molecules travel into the stroma, where the Calvin cycle reactions take place [ev_007]. The Calvin cycle then uses the energy in ATP and NADPH to convert molecules into G3P, which is used to build carbohydrate molecules [ev_010]. After the energy is released, the \"empty\" carriers return to the light-dependent reactions to obtain more energy [ev_001] [ev_005].",
    "short_answer": "Light-dependent reactions store light energy in ATP and NADPH, which travel to the stroma and supply the energy the Calvin cycle uses to build carbohydrates [ev_007] [ev_010].",
